+This is a pure-Python reimplementation of `packignore`, the legacy exclusion
+engine. docs/PACKIGNORE.md describes the basics; the rules below are what the
+engine actually does, including the parts the docs leave out.
+
+Reading .packignore files
+    Lines are split with str.splitlines() (so \\r, \\v, \\f, \\x1c-\\x1e, \\x85,
+    \\u2028 and \\u2029 all end a line) and stripped with str.strip(). Empty
+    lines and lines starting with ";" are skipped. "#" is NOT a comment.
+    A leading "!" negates the rest of the line (which is not stripped again).
+    There are no escapes and no character classes: "\\" and "[" are literal.
+
+Patterns
+    A trailing "/" makes the pattern match directories only. Leading "/"s
+    are dropped; if the pattern had one, or still contains a "/", it is
+    anchored to the directory holding the .packignore, otherwise it matches
+    a name at any depth below it. A path segment of exactly "**" matches zero
+    or more segments; elsewhere "*" (including "**", "***", ...) matches any
+    run of characters within a segment and "?" exactly one character.
+
+Deciding a file
+    Every level of the file's path (each ancestor directory, then the file
+    itself) is checked. The deepest level that any rule matches decides.
+    Among the rules matching that level, the winner is the one from the
+    deepest .packignore; then the one with the most literal characters
+    (anything but "*", "?" and "/"); then the later line. The file is
+    excluded if the winner is a positive (non-"!") rule.
+
+    Before any .packignore rule, two implicit file-level rules exclude
+    executable files (any x bit) and files whose first line contains
+    "@generated". They rank below every real rule, so "!*" overrides them.
+
+    A .packignore inside a directory that is itself excluded is never read.
